"[1109.6882] Verifying Computations with Streaming Interactive Proofs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.6882)\nciteturn6view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.6882\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 173\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Databases\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 30 Sep 2011]\nL9: # Title:Verifying Computations with Streaming Interactive Proofs\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Graham Cormode】, 【6†Justin Thaler】, 【7†Ke Yi】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Verifying Computations with Streaming Interactive Proofs, by Graham Cormode and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】\nL16: > Abstract:When computation is outsourced, the data owner would like to be assured that the desired computation has been performed correctly by the service provider. In theory, proof systems can give the necessary assurance, but prior work is not sufficiently scalable or practical.\nL17: In this paper, we develop new proof protocols for verifying computations which are streaming in nature: the verifier (data owner) needs only logarithmic space and a single pass over the input, and after observing the input follows a simple protocol with a prover (service provider) that takes logarithmic communication spread over a logarithmic number of rounds. These ensure that the computation is performed correctly: that the service provider has not made any errors or missed out some data.\nL18: The guarantee is very strong: even if the service provider deliberately tries to cheat, there is only vanishingly small probability of doing so undetected, while a correct computation is always accepted. We first observe that some theoretical results can be modified to work with streaming verifiers, showing that there are efficient protocols for problems in the complexity classes NP and NC.\nL19: Our main results then seek to bridge the gap between theory and practice by developing usable protocols for a variety of problems of central importance in streaming and database processing. All these problems require linear space in the traditional streaming model, and therefore our protocols demonstrate that adding a prover can exponentially reduce the effort needed by the verifier. Our experimental results show that our protocols are practical and scalable.\nL20: Comments:  | VLDB2012\nL21: Subjects:  | Databases (cs.DB)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【9†arXiv:1109.6882】 [cs.DB]\nL23:    | (or 【10†arXiv:1109.6882v1】 [cs.DB] for this version)\nL24:    | 【11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1109.6882†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: Journal reference:  | Proceedings of the VLDB Endowment (PVLDB), Vol. 5, No. 1, pp. 25-36 (2011)\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Graham Cormode [【12†view email】] [via Ahmet Sacan as proxy]\nL29: [v1] Fri, 30 Sep 2011 16:24:53 UTC (341 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Verifying Computations with Streaming Interactive Proofs, by Graham Cormode and 2 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * 【8†View PDF】\nL38: \nL39: 【13†view license】\nL40: \nL41: ### Additional Features\nL42: \nL43:   * 【14†Audio Summary】\nL44: ### Current browse context:\nL45: \nL46: cs.DB\nL47: \nL48: 【15†< prev】   |   【16†next >】\nL49: \nL50: 【17†new】 | 【18†recent】 | 【19†2011-09】\nL51: \nL52: Change to browse by:\nL53: \nL54: 【20†cs】\nL55: \nL56: ### References & Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * 【21†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL59:   * 【22†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL60:   * 【23†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL61: ### 【24†DBLP†dblp.uni-trier.de】 - CS Bibliography\nL62: \nL63: 【25†listing†dblp.uni-trier.de】 | 【26†bibtex†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL64: \nL65: 【27†Graham Cormode†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL66: 【28†Justin Thaler†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL67: 【29†Ke Yi†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL68: \nL69: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL70: \nL71: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL72: \nL73: [Button: ×]\nL74: \nL75: loading...\nL76: \nL77: Data provided by:\nL78: \nL79: ### Bookmark\nL80: \nL81: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL82: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL83: \nL84: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL85: \nL86: Bibliographic Explorer (【30†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL87: \nL88: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL89: \nL90: Connected Papers (【31†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com】)\nL91: \nL92: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL93: \nL94: Litmaps (【32†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co】)\nL95: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nECCC - TR22-093 (https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2022/093/)\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2022/093/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\nL0: 【0†Image: Electronic Colloquium on Computational Complexity】\nL1: \nL2: Under the auspices of the 【1†Computational Complexity Foundation (CCF)†computationalcomplexity.org】\nL3: \nL4: [Input] [Input]\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Image: |】 【2†Image: |】\nL7: \nL8: 【3†Image: icon】 【4†Image: Submit Paper】\nL9:   *   *   *     *     * 【5†2026】\nL10:     * 【6†2025】\nL11:     * 【7†2024】\nL12:     * 【8†2026...1994】\nL13:     *   *   *     *     *     *     *     *     *   *     * 【9†Call for Papers】\nL14:     * 【10†How to use】\nL15:     * 【11†Scientific Board】\nL16:     * 【12†Local Office】\nL17:     * 【13†Copyright Notice】\nL18:     * 【14†History】\nL19:   *     *     *     *     *     *     *   *     *     *     *     *     *     *   * * * *\nL20: \nL21: REPORTS > DETAIL:\nL22: \nL23: * * *\nL24: ### Revision(s):\nL25: \nL26: Revision #4 to TR22-093 | 8th January 2023 03:30\nL27: #### More Verifier Efficient Interactive Protocols For Bounded Space\nL28: \nL29: 【15†Image: Contact】\nL30: \nL31: 【16†Image: Add Comment】\nL32: \nL33: 【17†Revision #4】 Authors: 【18†Joshua Cook】\nL34: Accepted on: 8th January 2023 03:30\nL35: Downloads: 736\nL36: \nL37: Keywords:\nL38: \nL39: Abstract:\nL40: Let $\\mathbf{TISP}[T, S]$, $\\mathbf{BPTISP}[T, S]$, $\\mathbf{NTISP}[T, S]$ and $\\mathbf{coNTISP}[T, S]$ be the set of languages recognized by deterministic, randomized, nondeterministic, and co-nondeterministic algorithms, respectively, running in time $T$ and space $S$. Let $\\mathbf{ITIME}[T_V, T_P]$ be the set of languages recognized by an interactive protocol where the verifier runs in time $T_V$ and the prover runs in time $T_P$.\nL41: For $S = \\Omega(\\log(n))$ and $T$ constructible in time $\\log(T) S + n$, we prove:\nL42: $$\\mathbf{TISP}[T, S] \\subseteq \\mathbf{ITIME}[\\tilde{O}(\\log(T) S + n), 2^{O(S)}]$$\nL43: $$\\mathbf{BPTISP}[T, S] \\subseteq \\mathbf{ITIME}[\\tilde{O}(\\log(T) S + n), 2^{O(S)}]$$\nL44: $$\\mathbf{NTISP}[T, S] \\subseteq \\mathbf{ITIME}[\\tilde{O}(\\log(T)^2 S + n), 2^{O(S)}]$$\nL45: $$\\mathbf{coNTISP}[T, S] \\subseteq \\mathbf{ITIME}[\\tilde{O}(\\log(T)^2 S + n), 2^{O(S)}] .$$\nL46: The best prior verifier time is from Shamir:\nL47: $$\\mathbf{TISP}[T, S] \\subseteq \\mathbf{ITIME}[\\tilde{O}(\\log(T)(S + n)), 2^{O(\\log(T)(S + n))}]. $$\nL48: Our prover is faster, and our verifier is faster when $S = o(n)$.\nL49: The best prior prover time uses ideas from Goldwasser, Kalai, and Rothblum:\nL50: $$\\mathbf{NTISP}[T, S] \\subseteq \\mathbf{ITIME}[\\tilde{O}(\\log(T) S^2 + n), 2^{O(S)}].$$\nL51: Our verifier is faster when $\\log(T) = o(S)$, and for deterministic algorithms.\nL52: To our knowledge, no previous interactive protocol for $\\mathbf{TISP}$ simultaneously has the same verifier time and prover time as ours. In our opinion, our protocol is also simpler than previous protocols.\nL53: Changes to previous version:\nL54: Fixed a slight bug in the claimed performance of the nondeterministic protocol case. We claimed the verifier ran in time\nL55: log(T)^2 S + n\nL56: but only showed a verifier which ran in time\nL57: log(T)^2 S + log(T) n.\nL58: This can be a large difference for NSPACE[n^{1/3}]. I have now updated the paper to prove the stronger claim. This required updating the arithemtization to include a more space efficient, but less time efficient variation, and requires using a second interactive protocol for nondeterministic algorithms to avoid calculating M^ directly with a large field.\nL59: I also added an efficient arithmetization of multitape Turing Machines to the appendix. This is (by constant factors) less efficient than arithmetizing the RAM algorithm directly, but the simpler instruction set allows a more complete proof than provided for RAM algorithms. This is probably what I should have used in the main paper, if I would have proved it earlier.\nL60: \nL61: Revision #3 to TR22-093 | 26th October 2022 00:36\nL62: #### More Verifier Efficient Interactive Protocols For Bounded Space\nL63: \nL64: 【19†Revision #3】 Authors: 【18†Joshua Cook】\nL65: Accepted on: 26th October 2022 00:36\nL66: Downloads: 1142\nL67: \nL68: Keywords:\nL69: \nL70: Abstract:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCMU CSD PhD Blog - Algorithms for the Sum-check Protocol (https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~csd-phd-blog/2026/sumcheck-proving/)\nciteturn6view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~csd-phd-blog/2026/sumcheck-proving/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 177\nL0:   * 【0†Home】\nL1:   * 【1†Areas】\nL2:   * 【2†Tags】\nL3:   * 【3†RSS】\nL4:   * 【4†CSD†csd.cmu.edu】\nL5: \nL6: 【5†Image: CSD Logo】\nL7: \nL8: 【6†CSD PhD Blog】\nL9: \nL10:   * 【0†Home】\nL11:   * 【1†Areas】\nL12:   * 【2†Tags】\nL13:   * 【3†RSS】\nL14:   * 【4†CSD†csd.cmu.edu】\nL15: ## Contents\nL16: \nL17:   * 【7†How to Verify Private (or Expensive) Computations】\nL18:   * 【8†Sum-Check Protocol Overview】\nL19:   * 【9†Brief Interlude on Multilinear Polynomials】\nL20:     * 【10†Example: Arithmetizing a Batched Zero-Check】\nL21:   * 【11†Existing Algorithms for Sum-Check】\nL22:     * 【12†Linear-time algorithm】\nL23:     * 【13†Streaming algorithm with logarithmic space】\nL24:   * 【14†New Idea: Round Batching and its Benefits】\nL25:     * 【15†Why does round batching help?】\nL26:   * 【16†Conclusion】\nL27:   * 【17†Citations:】\nL28: # 【18†Algorithms for the Sum-check Protocol】\nL29: \nL30: 2026-09-01 | 【19†Quang Dao†quangvdao.github.io】\nL31: ## How to Verify Private (or Expensive) Computations\nL32: \nL33: Suppose a hospital runs a statistical analysis over millions of patient records to determine whether a drug is effective. The computation takes days of cluster time, and the raw data cannot be shared due to privacy regulations. A regulatory agency needs to trust the result—but cannot re-run the computation or inspect the data. How can they be confident the analysis was performed correctly?\nL34: This is an instance of a broader challenge: we increasingly rely on computational results we cannot feasibly re-check. Sometimes the data is private, so other parties cannot re-run the computation at all. Other times the data is public but the computation is simply too expensive to repeat.\nL35: Cryptographic zero-knowledge proof systems address this dilemma. They let an untrusted prover convince a verifier that some private data satisfies a public statement. For instance, you can prove that you are over 18 or that your account has sufficient balance without revealing your birthdate or financial history. Proofs are short and fast to verify—milliseconds rather than hours or days—so the savings compound when many parties need to check the same result.\nL36: Over the past decade, cryptographic proof systems have matured from theoretical curiosities into practical tools, with applications ranging from age verification [1] to blockchain scalability [2]. However, generating a proof is still orders of magnitude slower than running the computation natively, making prover efficiency a central challenge.\nL37: Many state-of-the-art proof systems owe much of their performance to a classical protocol from 1992: the sum-check protocol [4]. Sum-check’s very success, however, has made it a bottleneck: it often consumes the majority of the total proving time. In this blog post, I will introduce the sum-check protocol, detail well-known algorithms for its prover, and present a new technique that speeds up the prover in practical settings [9].\nL38: ## Sum-Check Protocol Overview\nL39: \nL40: The sum-check protocol [4] is an interactive proof that allows an untrusted prover to convince a computationally limited verifier of the value of a very large sum. Informally, the verifier wants to check a sum of many evaluations of a multivariate polynomial over a large product domain, but would like to avoid explicitly computing all of those values.\nL41: We will require two properties from the protocol: completeness (an honest prover always convinces the verifier) and soundness (a cheating prover is caught with overwhelming probability). After describing the protocol, we will verify that sum-check satisfies both.\nL42: Formally, we fix a finite field \\( \\mathbb{F} \\) (a number system with addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division, but only finitely many elements) and a multivariate polynomial \\( p(X_1, \\dots, X_n) \\in \\mathbb{F}[X_1,\\dots, X_n] \\), of degree bounded by \\( d \\) in each variable. The sum-check claim is then $$ \\sum_{x_1 \\in H_1, \\dots, x_n \\in H_n} p(x_1,\\dots,x_n) = c, $$ for some evaluation domains \\( H_1, \\dots, H_n \\subseteq \\mathbb{F} \\) and a claimed value \\( c \\in \\mathbb{F} \\).\n"
